# Requests two-node development increment

Root decision, 2026-09-14. This adds a natural repository trajectory to the
development integration program. It does not replace the four-family,
cache-state and one-factor program in `../next_stage_protocol.md`, enroll a
confirmatory family, or close missing mechanisms by omitting them.

The trajectory uses the public Requests 5414 base followed by the public 6028
base. Their forward ancestry has been checked. Public issue bytes, base source,
permitted production paths, context inventories and scorer profiles must be
frozen before effects. Reference patches, benchmark test patches, test identities
and raw scorer logs remain outside the proposer and context-service input.
Only sanitized grades, counts and execution status may return from scoring.

An accepted first-node candidate becomes an actual Git branch whose parent is
the first public base. At the second node, an explicit upstream-update event
three-way merges that accepted branch with the second public base. The result
must retain accepted edits, or record actual merge conflicts. Conflict repair
uses the same model budget as other reasoning. Unresolved conflicts remain
failures or pending work. A failed first-node attempt retains its outcome and
cost; later public upstream source may advance the input state but cannot
retroactively change the earlier outcome. A reference patch is never supplied
to make a failed branch continue successfully.

The first cold tranche contains four trajectories, one per intervention:

| Arm | Development intervention |
|---|---|
| A | Ordinary lexical source retrieval, common cheap current-source checks, permitted public history, metered residual and durable effect identities. |
| B | A with qualified native semantic substitution and equal source-expansion access. Unsupported source remains byte-identical raw context. The currently qualified Requests profile permits zero omissions, so this may be an identity control for context content. |
| C | B plus actual native IPA, eligible bounded CEGIS and routing before the common residual. Unsupported obligations remain visible. |
| Partial D | C plus actual qualified memory consumption and native accepted-source publication/recovery. Lifecycle reuse, certified procedure execution and integrated theorem admission remain missing until their real consumers are added and measured. |

Use the existing qualified Grok 4.6 low-effort provider profile, with
`max_tokens=2048` and a 240-second transport deadline. Each trajectory has at
most four provider requests, including all planning, formalization, expansion,
proposal, review, retry and merge-resolution requests. Node 1 may consume at
most two; unused requests remain available to node 2. There are at most sixteen
provider requests in this first cold tranche. Exact replacement edits
(`path`, `old`, `new`) may express patches within the common output bound;
ambiguous replacement, unauthorized paths or invalid output must be rejected
and charged. No baseline request is forced when a common check already closes
the current obligation.

The executable freeze must specify arm order, prompts, retrieval/expansion and
edit rules, source/service/profile hashes, candidate acceptance, call identities,
timeouts and terminal failure policy before dispatch. Every possible provider
effect has a durable intent; unavailable remote usage remains unknown and is
never imputed as zero or retried invisibly. Qualified context preparation,
priming, full grader replays, publication and recovery costs must be retained
alongside model usage. Independent audit-only work is a separate denominator.

Before live dispatch, check exact objective bytes (including CRLF), accepted
source carry, the failed-first-node branch, bounded edit application, complete
source inventory/modes, unknown-effect behavior and repository-specific native
publication admission. The scorer must pass its actual versioned control run;
standalone environment controls alone are insufficient. Endpoint v2's failed
reference is retained, and endpoint v3's single frozen four-control run passes
under its named readiness policy. The latter does not prove the cause of the
former failure or guarantee future network stability.

This is a development and telemetry qualification on one already exposed
repository family. Report both node outcomes, every invocation and all failed
expenditure. There is no independent-family uncertainty estimate from one
trajectory family, and zero expenditure on unresolved tasks is not a token
saving at adequate quality. The separately screened Rebench families remain
reserved for later source/environment/scorer and split qualification.
